{"command": "cat > /tmp/spec_update.py << 'PYEOF'\nimport io, sys\n\np = \"/testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md\"\ns = open(p, encoding=\"utf-8\").read()\n\ndef rep(old, new, count=1):\n    global s\n    n = s.count(old)\n    assert n >= count, (\"anchor not found (%d):\" % n, old[:80])\n    s = s.replace(old, new, count)\n\n# 1. Status line\nrep(\n\"**Status**: Draft (updated for milestone 2 and Claim-A extensions R05b / R05 clarification)\",\n\"**Status**: Draft (updated for milestones 1-3, including Claim-A extensions R05b / R05 clarification)\")\n\n# 2. User Story 4 before Edge Cases\nus4 = \"\"\"### User Story 4 - Idempotent batch replay with a token (Priority: P2)\n\nA caller submits the same batch more than once (for example, after a retry) and\nwants a repeated submission to be a no-op that returns the original result\ninstead of re-running the mutations. The caller supplies a nonempty string\nidempotency token to apply(). The first successful run records the token with\nthe batch and its inserted IDs; a later call with the same token and an\nidentical batch returns a detached copy of the original inserted IDs without\nrerunning any mutation, even if other direct database writes occurred in the\nmeantime. Reusing a token with different operations is an error that leaves the\ndata unchanged. A batch that fails does not consume its token, so the same\ntoken can be retried after the failure is fixed. Omitting the token (None)\nmeans normal, non-idempotent operation.\n\n**Why this priority**: Idempotency lets callers retry batches safely (e.g. after\na dropped response) without duplicating inserts; it is a core reliability\nguarantee layered on top of the atomic apply.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by applying a batch with a token,\napplying the identical batch with the same token again (optionally after a\ndirect db.insert), asserting the second call returns the same IDs and the table\nis unchanged; then asserting a different batch with the same token raises\nValueError and leaves the table unchanged.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a successful apply() of a batch with a nonempty string token,\n   **When** the identical batch is applied again with the same token (even after\n   other direct database writes), **Then** a detached copy of the original\n   inserted IDs is returned and no mutation is rerun (table contents unchanged\n   by the replay).\n2. **Given** a token already recorded by a successful batch, **When** a\ndifferent batch is applied with that same token, **Then** a ValueError is\n   raised and the default table is unchanged.\n3. **Given** a batch that fails, **When** it is applied with a token, **Then**\n   the token is NOT consumed and the same token may be reused on a later\n   attempt; the failed batch still rolls back per R02.\n4. **Given** apply() called with a token that is not a nonempty string (None is\n   the only other allowed value), **When** it is called, **Then** a ValueError\n   is raised and the table is unchanged.\n\n---\n\n### Edge Cases\"\"\"\nrep(\"### Edge Cases\", us4)\n\n# 3. Token edge cases before Requirements\nedge_add = \"\"\"- Token replay after other direct writes: the replay still returns the\n  original inserted IDs and does not rerun the batch.\n- Token reused with different operations: ValueError, table unchanged.\n- A failed batch with a token: token not consumed; retry with same token works\n  once the batch is valid.\n- Token that is empty string, non-string, or None-with-operations: None is the\n  normal no-token path; empty/non-string tokens raise ValueError.\n- Mutating a list returned by a token replay: must not expose or corrupt the\n  writer's internal token bookkeeping.\n\n## Requirements *(mandatory)*\"\"\"\nrep(\"## Requirements *(mandatory)*\", edge_add)\n\n# 4. FR-015/FR-016 before Key Entities\nfr = \"\"\"- **FR-015** (R07): apply() MUST accept an optional idempotency token\n  (default None). A token MUST be a nonempty string; any other non-None value\n  MUST raise ValueError. Repeating an already successful batch with the same\n  token and an identical operations list MUST return a detached copy of the\n  original inserted IDs without rerunning any mutation, even if other direct\n  database writes occurred since. Reusing a token with a different operations\n  list MUST raise ValueError and MUST NOT change the data. A failed batch MUST\n  NOT consume its token. token=None MUST mean normal, non-idempotent\n  operation.\n- **FR-016** (R08): Token bookkeeping MUST belong to the BatchWriter instance\n  and MUST NOT be stored in user documents. preview() MUST NOT record or\n  consume a token and MUST keep its original single-argument signature. Lists\n  returned by apply() or preview() MUST NOT expose the writer's internal token\n  bookkeeping through mutation.\n\n### Key Entities\"\"\"\nrep(\"### Key Entities\", fr)\n\n# 5. SC-010/SC-011 before Assumptions\nsc = \"\"\"- **SC-010**: 100% of repeated identical batch/token submissions return\n  the original inserted IDs (a detached copy) and rerun 0 mutations, even when\n  other direct database writes occurred in between; a token reused with\ndifferent operations raises ValueError in 100% of cases with no data change.\n- **SC-011**: 0 token-leak faults: token bookkeeping never appears in user\n  documents, preview() never records/consumes a token and keeps its original\n  signature, and mutating any returned list never exposes or corrupts internal\n  token state.\n\n## Assumptions\"\"\"\nrep(\"## Assumptions\", sc)\n\n# 6. Assumptions A10/A11 after A9 (end of file)\na9 = \"\"\"- **A9** (from R05 clarification): \"Writing the next-insertion-ID state back\n  to the underlying table\" means the table's own next-ID bookkeeping (used by\n  ordinary inserts) reflects the highest ID consumed by any successful batch,\n  so direct db.insert continues the sequence; no separate persistent\n  counter file or new storage format is required.\"\"\"\nassert a9 in s, \"A9 anchor not found\"\ns = s.replace(a9, a9 + \"\"\"\n- **A10** (from R07): \"Identical batch\" for token replay means the operations\n  argument is equal to the operations list recorded for that token on its first\n  successful run (compared by value, not identity); equality is evaluated on\n  the validated, normalized operation data. The recorded inserted IDs are the\n  return value of the first successful run.\n- **A11** (from R07/R08): Token bookkeeping is in-memory state on the\n  BatchWriter instance (not persisted to the database or to user documents),\n  so a new BatchWriter instance has no knowledge of prior tokens; this matches\n  the instance-scoped nature of the requirement. The public acceptance test\n  reuses the same BatchWriter instance for the replay.\"\"\".rstrip(\"\\n\"))\n\nopen(p, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\").write(s)\nprint(\"spec updated OK, lines:\", s.count(chr(10))+1)\nPYEOF\npython3 /tmp/spec_update.py", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "spec updated OK, lines: 354\n"}